TRACKING DATA — a derivation is distributed here

  The files under data/ are committed so the hosted app works. They are not
  either source: each is one passage of play, resampled to 10 Hz, with player
  names and squad numbers stripped by tools/anonymise.py. Neither source
  publishes a licence file. The raw releases are not redistributed - the
  scripts in tools/ fetch them from the publishers.

  Two of the football passages carry a small repair to the ball's path, each
  recorded in the file itself. In one, the tracker lost sight of the ball for
  0.9 s during a tackle and the path is drawn straight between the two measured
  positions, 4.7 m apart - meta.bridged. In the other, the tracker jumped to a
  different object and picked the ball up 3.8 m away in a single frame; the
  offset is eased backwards over the preceding 1.8 s so the ball keeps the shape
  of its measured motion instead of darting - meta.mended.
  Both are repairs of tracking artefacts, not authored movement.

  One of them is partly modelled. data/play_fieldgoal.json is a record-distance
  field goal, and no such kick exists in the tracking: the release covers one
  game, whose longest made field goal is 32 yards. The players and the strike
  are that measured attempt, moved bodily down the field; the distance and the
  ball's flight are computed by tools/fieldgoal.py. The file records this in
  meta.modelled and the app reads it out in the replay caption, so nothing
  claims to be measured that is not.

